description.narrative:
The overall objective of the intervention is to develop an effective and sustainable framework for preventing and supressing money laundering and terrorist financing in Serbia. The overall objective is broken down into 7 outcomes, as follows:
Outcome 1: Strengthened compliance and oversight of AML/CFT financial and designated non-financial business and professions (DNFBPs);
Outcome 2: Enhanced Non-Profit Organizations (NPO) terrorist-financing risk management;
Outcome 3: Developed risk-based border controls related to cash and goods smuggling, terrorist financing, and proliferation;
Outcome 4: Increased transparency of beneficial ownership (BO) information for legal persons, arrangements and trusts;
Outcome 5: Enhanced criminal justice sector processing of ML/FT cases;
Outcome 6: Strengthened effectiveness in the confiscation of proceeds and instrumentalities of crime;
Outcome 7: Enhanced inter-agency cooperation on verification of asset declarations.

The key results achieved by this program have been presented on three levels: policy, legislative, and capacity building.

On policy level, the project has achieved the following results:

- National and sectoral risks have been assessed through expert support. The new National ML/TF Risk Assessment of Serbia (NRA) and four sectoral risk assessments have been developed. The results of these assessments have informed the strategic response to the prevention and repression of money laundering and terrorism financing. A comprehensive gap assessment has been conducted to assess the effectiveness of implementation of preventive measures and supervision in both financial and non-financial sectors and a detailed map of actions to address the deficiencies was agreed with
beneficiaries.

- Multiple protocols of cooperation between the National Bank of Serbia, the Agency for the Prevention of Corruption and the APML have been fostered to improve the exchange of information and financial intelligence.

- On aggregate level, the project has contributed to the full implementation of 28 measures from the Action Plan for the implementation of the National Strategy for Prevention of Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Proliferation financing (2020-2024), enhancing the effective application of various AML/CFT measures.

On legislative level, the project has achieved the following results:

- The new draft Law on Beneficial Ownership Registry has been drafted and presented for public debate in August 2023. It is still pending adoption;

- Recommendations for amendments to the Criminal Code, Criminal Procedure Code, and the Law on Seizure and Confiscation of Criminal Proceeds have been prepared, aiming to remove obstacles for effective prosecution of ML offences and confiscation of illicit proceeds. These recommendations are used as the basis for the review of criminal legislation, to be submitted for adoption by the end of 2024;

- Through a coordinated effort with other donors, recommendations for improvements have been prepared regarding the General Mandatory Instruction of the Supreme Public Prosecutor's Office on the Use of the Financial Intelligence to Initiate Proceedings for the Criminal Offences of ML and TF. A working group is currently amending the instruction based on these recommendations and its adoption is expected by September.

- The project has produced recommendations for amendments of the Law on Associations and the Law on Foundations and Endowments, with a view to enable a more accurate registration and supervision of NPOs.

On capacity building level, the project has achieved the following results:

- More than 400 supervisors, representatives of law enforcement agencies, prosecution and reporting entities have been trained in risk-based approach to supervision, compliance, integration of findings of risk assessments in their work and detection and reporting of ML/TF;

- A comprehensive training program has been developed, encompassing three modules on processing and adjudicating standalone money laundering. 30 prosecutors, judges, and police officers have been trained;

- 14 judges, prosecutors and police officers have participated in peer exchange that encompassed trainings on criminal origin of assets in court procedures, tax evasion schemes used as predicate offence to ML, and practice in combating terrorism financing with foreign elements;

- Expert assistance has been provided for the development and adoption of sector-specific procedures, such as sectoral indicators for identifying suspicious transactions for auditors; indicators for illicit transfer of cash, Bearer Negotiable Instruments (BNIs) and the dual use goods for customs officers and border police; guidelines on reporting of suspicious activities for reporting entities and supervisors; guidance for the update and creation of new risk indicators for  virtual assets, virtual assets service providers and money and value transfer
services (MVTS).;

- A number of existing supervisory methodologies and operational procedures have been reviewed and upgraded with the assistance of the project including the methodologies for ongoing "fit and proper" verification of owners, managers, and compliance officers in the banking sector and virtual assets service providers;

- A methodology and procedures have been developed and used to train 25 customs officers and border police on the effective application of risk-based border controls;

- The authorities and civil society have been involved in joint discussions, trust-building initiatives and development of proportionate oversight procedures;

- The representatives of 15 NPO have been trained to recognise and mitigate risks of abuse for terrorism financing. A working group for the supervision of NPOs has been trained in implementation of improved supervision procedures;

- IT capacities of the beneficiaries for financial investigations have been strengthened, by initiating the development of the financial investigation software and providing supporting hardware.
